Find the number of rectangles that can be formed inside a fixed regular dodecagon (<imath>12</imath>-gon) where each side of the rectangle lies on either a side or a diagonal of the dodecagon. The diagram below shows three of those rectangles.

<asy>
/* Made by Mathemagician108 */
unitsize(0.6 inch);
for(int i=0; i<360; i+=30) {
dot(dir(i), 4+black);
draw(dir(i)--dir(i+30));
}
draw(dir(120)--dir(330));
filldraw(dir(210)--dir(240)--dir(30)--dir(60)--cycle, mediumgray, linewidth(1.5));
draw((0,0.366)--(0.366,0), linewidth(1.5));
</asy>

solution:
By Furaken

There are two kinds of such rectangles: those whose sides are parallel to some edges of the regular 12-gon (Case 1), and those whose sides are not (Case 2).

For Case 1, WLOG assume that the rectangle's sides are horizontal and vertical (don't forget to multiply by 3 at the end of Case 1). Then the rectangle's sides coincide with these segments as shown in the diagram.
