solution:
One way of approaching this is drawing the next circle of boxes around the current square.
<asy>
filldraw((-1,-1)--(6,-1)--(6,6)--(-1,6)--cycle,mediumgray,black);
filldraw((0,0)--(5,0)--(5,5)--(0,5)--cycle,white,black);
filldraw((1,1)--(4,1)--(4,4)--(1,4)--cycle,mediumgray,black);
filldraw((2,2)--(3,2)--(3,3)--(2,3)--cycle,white,black);
draw((4,-1)--(4,6));
draw((3,-1)--(3,6));
draw((2,-1)--(2,6));
draw((1,-1)--(1,6));
draw((-1,4)--(6,4));
draw((-1,3)--(6,3));
draw((-1,2)--(6,2));
draw((-1,1)--(6,1));
draw((0,-1)--(0,6));
draw((-1,5)--(6,5));
draw((-1,0)--(6,0));
draw((5,-1)--(5,6));
</asy>
We can now count the number of black and white tiles; 32 black tiles and 17 white tiles. This means the answer is <math>\boxed{\textbf{(D) }32:17}</math>.

